WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.083 --> 00:00:07.371
This episode is sponsored by 80,000 Hours. 80,000&nbsp;
Hours is a nonprofit that aims to help people have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.371 --> 00:00:13.680
a positive impact with their career. Head to&nbsp;
80000hours.org/microcosmos to start planning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.609
a career that could potentially help solve&nbsp;
one of the world’s most pressing problems.

00:00:38.247 --> 00:00:43.120
From our vantage point, as relatively large&nbsp;
organisms, it can be easy to overlook the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:49.920
microcosmos, because it is simply too small&nbsp;
to see. It floats in front of our eyes at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:56.568
all times, and yet we cannot make out&nbsp;
any of the details until we turn to other tools.&nbsp;

00:00:56.568 --> 00:01:00.742
And then, even when we do turn to those&nbsp;
tools, it can be easy to overlook the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.742 --> 00:01:06.640
parts of the microcosmos that seem relatively&nbsp;
understated compared to all of the wondrous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:13.040
dramatic events that can take place. After all, if&nbsp;
you’ve just come from, say, watching a tardigrade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:19.503
wandering through a maze of moss…you might find&nbsp;
something like this a bit underwhelming.

00:01:19.503 --> 00:01:25.600
There’s just some squishy dots, floating around,&nbsp;
not doing anything particularly noteworthy except

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.840
for a few brief flashes of movement. The movement&nbsp;
is so quick that you might see it and just move

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.567
on to something else, like when you glimpse&nbsp;
a bird flying out of the corner of your eye.

00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:42.000
It captures your attention for a second,&nbsp;
and perhaps releases it just as quickly.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.760
But for today, because we are us, we're gonna
linger&nbsp;on that moment and let it capture our&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:53.631
attention a little longer. Because the organism 
you’re&nbsp;looking at now is a ciliate called Cyclidium,

00:01:53.631 --> 00:01:59.520
which is known for its ability to dart straight&nbsp;
through the microcosmos like an arrow. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.968
only a few micrometers in length, but packed&nbsp;
in its small body is the ability to detect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.968 --> 00:02:11.280
threats and quickly jump out of their reach.
This is not the first time we’ve seen a very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:17.200
very fast-moving ciliate on this channel&nbsp;
before. Early on, we watched as bundles of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.320
the bell-shaped Vorticella extended&nbsp;
their long stalks and then rapidly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:28.005
contracted those stalks into tightly-packed&nbsp;
spirals when other organisms got too close.&nbsp;

00:02:28.579 --> 00:02:35.200
We’ve also watched a Spirostomum quickly evade&nbsp;
unwanted contact, pulling back and shrinking down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:41.360
to get itself out of reach as quickly as possible.
We can think of the vorticella and spirostomum as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.800
doing something that’s more like flinching, a&nbsp;
rapid contraction that moves them away from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.800
potential threat, but that doesn’t necessarily&nbsp;
transport them to a whole new location.&nbsp;

00:02:52.248 --> 00:02:58.560
It’s remarkable and requires both tremendous&nbsp;
force and speed. But ciliates like the cyclidium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:04.560
we were watching earlier or these Halteria are&nbsp;
performing a very different sort of athleticism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.520
something, more like a long jump.
Not that they look like they should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:14.480
setting any records. Halteria are only&nbsp;
around 20 microns in length, and most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:21.311
the time they just kind of spin, like a kid on&nbsp;
a teacup ride with no concept of dizziness.&nbsp;

00:03:21.311 --> 00:03:25.680
And charmingly, the halteria is&nbsp;
edged in cilia that swirls with it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.998
unfurling as it moves, and then wrapping&nbsp;
back inward as the organism winds down.&nbsp;

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:35.280
Halteria like to feed on bacteria, and&nbsp;
they reproduce quickly. So sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.579
James, our master of microscopes, will&nbsp;
find thousands of them in a single drop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.579 --> 00:03:44.000
another set of squishy dots&nbsp;
that move in and out of focus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.379
sometimes even obscuring the other interesting&nbsp;
bits that James is trying to look at.&nbsp;

00:03:49.379 --> 00:03:55.840
But let’s zoom out, past the point where we can&nbsp;
see the defined lines of the Halteria’s cilia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:03.000
where it really is just one dot navigating a field&nbsp;
of debris. And see if you can keep up with it.&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.560
Most of the time, it’s not really a challenge.&nbsp;
It looks like a floater in your vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.840
a clear blob that just idles away.
But then, seemingly out of nowhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:20.560
it leaps across the screen and out of view,&nbsp;
potentially traveling as far as 100 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:26.370
its own body length in a single second. That&nbsp;
would be like if I, a 6 foot human&nbsp;being,

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:31.920
could leap 600 feet in a second.
In response, you might say something like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.120
“Okay, yeah, sure Hank, but you’re a human&nbsp;
being. Things are different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:41.776
in the microcosmos.” And yes, this is true.
Things really are different in the microcosmos.

00:04:41.776 --> 00:04:48.240
In fact, things are a lot more challenging. Organisms&nbsp;
like halteria are living in water, but because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:54.800
their small size, that water feels thick like&nbsp;
molasses. So, they have to rely on structures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:02.000
like cilia to help them swim and move quickly.
But even then, most organisms only travel around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:08.000
10 times their body length in a single second.&nbsp;
The fact that halteria and a select group of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:15.858
leaping ciliates are able to jump 100 times their&nbsp;
body length in that same second is incredible.&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:23.520
And we don’t know exactly what it is that makes these&nbsp;
impressive hops possible. But it does require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.760
a lot of energy, so it makes&nbsp;
sense that in its off moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.680
the halteria prefers to be a little more static.
And there are a few other members of this elite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:39.600
jumping ciliate group, like the Strombidium&nbsp;
you see here leaping around. It is very impressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.240
that James can even keep up with them.
And that of course is the point.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:49.040
Not to avoid James, the ciliates are not 
aware of him. But the goal of this movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:54.640
is to help the ciliate be overlooked, to&nbsp;
ensure that predators can’t find them.&nbsp;

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:59.600
And it works. The slightest brush of a&nbsp;
predator’s presence sends these ciliates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:05.360
into escape mode. In one experiment, researchers&nbsp;
set some rotifers upon a population of Halteria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:10.960
and found that only around 12% of them ended up&nbsp;
as rotifer food. Another leaping ciliate called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:17.274
Strobilidium was even more successful at evading&nbsp;
capture, with only 3% of them getting eaten.&nbsp;

00:06:17.722 --> 00:06:23.360
There is however one notable exception to this&nbsp;
predator evasion. While Halteria might be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:29.520
escape rotifers, they have a dedicated predator&nbsp;
called the Actinobolina, which we’ve talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:37.200
before. This tentacled, toxin-loaded ciliate could&nbsp;
go after anything it wanted. But for some reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:43.367
it dines exclusively on Halteria.
Why? And how?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:50.320
No clue. Maybe they just got lucky to live in an&nbsp;
environment that was rich enough in Halteria that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:56.518
through evolution, the Actinobolina was able&nbsp;
to learn how to sneak up on their cagey prey.&nbsp;

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:02.240
So perhaps it is not possible for them&nbsp;
to always be overlooked. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:07.920
someone will take a moment to appreciate them.&nbsp;
If they are unfortunate, it is the one predator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:14.560
that has managed to find a way around their&nbsp;
tricks. But sometimes it’s just us, slowing down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:20.902
just enough to catch a glimpse of something&nbsp;
so much faster… and so much smaller… than we are.

